    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x,y)=(k,k)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nFor $y\\ne0$, the Rational Root Theorem gives $\\frac{x}{y}=1$; the case $y=0$ gives $(0,0)$.",
      "worked": "If $y=0$, the equation gives $x^3=0$, so $x=0$.\n\nIf $y\\ne0$, let $t=\\frac{x}{y}\\in\\mathbb Q$. Dividing by $y^3$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}t^3+1&=2t^2\\\\t^3-2t^2+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $P(t)=t^3-2t^2+1$. By the Rational Root Theorem, its only possible rational roots are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$P(1)=0,\\qquad P(-1)=-2\\ne0.$$\nThus $t=1$, so $x=y$.\n\nConversely, for any integer $k$,\n$$k^3+k^3=2k^3=2k^2k.$$\nTherefore all integer solutions are $(x,y)=(k,k)$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
